---

The paper is clearly written, has a solid formal treatment of MAD (including a uniqueness proof and its equivalence to all-pairs shortest paths), and proposes a simple but principled quasimetric (`d_simple`) with supporting theory. The algorithms MadDist and TDMadDist are well specified, and the experimental suite is thoughtfully constructed with environments where ground-truth MAD is available or tightly approximated. Empirically, MadDist consistently outperforms strong and relevant baselines (QRL, Hilbert) on correlation- and ratio-based metrics and shows very high success in a challenging downstream planning benchmark (OGBench PointMaze).

The main weaknesses are conceptual and evaluative rather than “fatal”:

- The learning objective is structurally driven by trajectory index gaps `j−i`, which are upper bounds on MAD. There is no explicit mechanism to pull distances below those behavior-policy path lengths when shorter paths exist; the paper relies on the quasimetric’s triangle inequality and global sharing, but does not analyze when this suffices to recover true MAD versus merely encoding behavior distances. This is acknowledged but under-theorized.
- Evaluation focuses on correlation and coefficient-of-variation of predicted/true-distance ratios, not absolute MAD error or calibration. This makes it hard to judge how numerically accurate the learned MAD is versus being roughly proportional.
- There is no systematic study of how behavior policy quality or trajectory suboptimality affects the learned distances, an important question given the “arbitrary trajectories” motivation.
- TDMadDist often underperforms MadDist and QRL but receives little analysis, so the second algorithmic contribution is weaker.
- Some key details of the downstream planning setup (planning horizon, success thresholds) are missing from the main text/appendix as given, which reduces reproducibility and the strength of the planning claim.
- Baseline coverage, while including the most relevant prior MAD/metric methods, is narrow compared to the broader temporal-distance and representation-learning literature discussed in related work.

Comparing to calibration examples:

- The π³ and World-In-World papers (avg scores ~7–8) offer strong, clearly impactful contributions with some caveats around optimization, attribution of gains, and benchmark confounds, but overall their empirical/theoretical support is solid. The MAD paper feels somewhat less fully “closed” conceptually because the central gap between MAD-as-shortest-path and upper-bound-based supervision is not really analyzed, and absolute error is missing. This justifies a slightly lower score than those.
- The symmetry-increase paper (avg 6.7) is a deep, mathematically heavy contribution with strong theory but a theory–practice gap and limited practical validation. The MAD paper is somewhat similar in that the formal side is stronger than the practical grounding of its core claim, though its empirical work is broader. Overall quality feels in the same band or slightly lower because the main conceptual limitation (learning upper bounds vs true MAD) is directly tied to the core claim.
- The PBRR paper (avg 5.0) and the monotonicity paper (avg 2.0) both have more serious theory/practice or evaluation gaps than this submission: the monotonicity paper misses evaluation on the very property it claims to enforce and was clearly judged weak; PBRR has a stronger empirical story but its regret theory does not match the implemented algorithm. The MAD paper is technically cleaner and empirically more convincing than the monotonicity reject, and more coherent overall than PBRR, so it should be scored above ~5 and well above ~2.

On novelty, technical soundness, and clarity, this work is clearly above-accept-threshold. The main issues are missing analyses and somewhat overstated claims about “accurately learning MAD from arbitrary trajectories” without quantifying the gap to behavior distances. Those are significant but not disqualifying; they suggest an accept with room for strengthening rather than a clear reject.

Balancing against the calibration set, a reasonable calibrated position is a solid but not top-tier accept-level score.

