This is a good paper. The consolidated review is largely fair, and after checking the paper, I think the key strengths are real and the main weaknesses are meaningful but not paper-breaking.

First, the core contribution is legitimate and clearly present in the paper itself. The paper really does formalize a distinct setting: the environment updates regardless of whether the LLM has finished, with a default action if no valid action arrives in time (lines 52–60, 184–191). That is not just rhetoric; it is operationalized cleanly in the benchmark. The decomposition into cognitive load and time pressure is also real and useful (lines 147–160, 274–280). This makes the paper stronger than a generic “new benchmark + hybrid agent” submission.

Second, the central empirical pattern appears strong. The paper shows complementary failure modes of reactive vs planning systems, and the narrative in §4 is supported by the actual setup and reported trends (lines 374–400). AgileThinker’s dual-thread design is also more specific than a vague hybrid: the reactive thread explicitly uses partial planner output during the same environment step (lines 70–76, 259–267). So the paper’s main scientific message—single-paradigm agents fail for opposite reasons in non-pausing environments, and a mixed design helps—is convincing.

Third, the review’s main criticisms are also supported by the paper. The biggest one is mechanism isolation. The paper claims partial-trace sharing is the distinctive advance, but I do not see a clean ablation isolating no-sharing vs final-output-only vs partial-trace-sharing in the main DeepSeek experiments. The Gemini appendix result using final outputs (line 1375–1376 in grep) is suggestive, but it is not a direct controlled ablation of the core proposed mechanism. So the architecture claim is stronger than the causal evidence.

Similarly, the compute/resource concern is only partially addressed. The concurrent-vs-parallel experiment in App. C.5 is useful: it does support that the gains are not only from abundant parallel throughput (lines 1661–1670). But that still does not fully compute-match the architecture against alternative controls that spend similar total inference budget differently. So I agree with the review that the evidence firmly supports “hybridization helps,” but only moderately supports “this exact coordination mechanism is the key reason.”

The wall-clock criticism is also fair. The paper’s real wall-clock validation is at 6 minutes per step for the 8k-token regime (lines 543–548), and the linear fit includes a very large intercept, β = 334.55 s (lines 536–540). So the claim that token count is a useful controlled proxy within their stack is supported; the broader “hardware-agnostic practical relevance” language is somewhat overstated. This is a framing problem, not a fatal flaw.

On the other hand, I would not score it down too aggressively for that. The paper is stronger as a benchmark/systems paper for asynchronous reasoning in dynamic environments than as a direct real-world low-latency control paper, but in that narrower framing it is genuinely strong.

