Because GAR-Bench is central to the paper’s strongest claims about **multi-prompt interaction and compositional reasoning**, this is a serious validity threat. It is not proof of leakage, but it is much more than a nit. This concern is stronger than many ordinary “possible contamination” complaints because the same named dataset appears in both training-data construction and benchmark sourcing, with no obvious disjointness statement in the inspected text.

However, I do **not** think this rises to the “clear reject <3” threshold from the instructions. Why:

1. The paper still has substantial value from the **architecture ablations** and **non-GAR-Bench evaluations**.
2. The benchmark itself appears thoughtfully built and not obviously vacuous.
