The consolidated review is largely fair, and the paper text confirms its main synthesis.

The paper’s real strength is narrower than its abstract/introduction suggest. From the abstract alone, the claim “outperforming existing training-based and training-free counting methods” is plainly overstated relative to Table 1: on FSC-147, several training-based methods beat it clearly (LOCA 10.79, PseCo 13.05, SAFECount 14.32 vs. 16.25), and TFCAC also beats it among training-free methods that use extra models. The body text is somewhat more careful later, explicitly claiming best performance among “training-free SAM only methods” on FSC-147 and noting TFCAC is better on CARPK, so the review’s complaint is accurate but not fatal.

Technically, the paper does seem to contain a real idea rather than pure prompt-budget brute force. The move from image-embedding verification to output-token verification is supported by Table 5 (16.94 to 16.25 MAE), and Table 3 shows the specific TS-SS metric is modestly but consistently best over cosine/L2. So the strongest contribution is indeed the decoder/output-token verification inside a pure-SAM pipeline. That is a meaningful empirical insight.

At the same time, the review is right that the probabilistic prompt generator is not cleanly isolated. Table 4 mostly shows that more iterations/prompts improve MAE, from very poor low-budget settings up to the final 16.25, but there is no matched-budget comparison against simpler prompting schemes. So one of the two headline components is not causally pinned down as well as it should be.

Empirical support is decent but not exceptional. The paper has two benchmarks, a one-shot table, several ablations, and some qualitative analysis. That is enough to establish the method works, especially on CARPK where 4.68 MAE is genuinely strong. But the efficiency story is underdeveloped: there is one runtime number (1.69 s/image on FSC-147) and a statement that it is “more than twice as fast” than TFCAC, without controlled side-by-side timing or memory comparisons. The discussion section even awkwardly pairs “superior computational efficiency” with a note that scalability for real-time applications remains limited.

There is no fundamental flaw that collapses the paper below reject-floor territory. The method is coherent, ablations exist, results are real, and the core claim that SAM decoder-side representations help counting verification is supported. The weaknesses are about overclaiming, incomplete isolation of components, and under-supported efficiency claims—not invalidation.

Relative to calibration examples:

- This paper feels **stronger than SURE (4.4)** and probably **stronger than VocSim (3.0)** in acceptance-likelihood terms, because those had more serious issues around claim interpretation or benchmark framing relative to their contributions. TOKENCOUNT’s empirical contribution is narrower, but more straightforwardly useful.
- It feels **roughly in the band of MetaOCDN (5.0)** and **Flatter Tokens / SFDD (5.5)**: both are papers with a real idea, decent experiments, and meaningful but not fully convincing headline framing. TOKENCOUNT is similar in that the core contribution is useful, but one or two central claims are oversold and some causal/efficiency evidence is missing.
- It is **weaker than DISCO (6.7)** and **Dens3R (6.0)**, both of which had broader and more convincing empirical support despite their own overclaiming/conceptual gaps.
- It is also **weaker than VideoZoomer (5.5)** in overall completeness and breadth, though comparable in the pattern of “solid method, some overclaiming, efficiency story not fully nailed down.”

So I would place TOKENCOUNT in the **mid-5s**, not as a clear accept-quality paper, but also not as a weak reject. The contribution is real and useful, especially for SAM-only training-free counting, yet the manuscript currently overstates its general standing and leaves key methodological questions under-ablated.

