The consolidated review looks broadly fair and well calibrated to the paper.

The paper is clearly a real contribution, not a broken or under-specified submission. The core method is concrete and technically meaningful: Stage 1 uses hierarchical memory with a large global chunk and reset local memories to break sequential dependence and enable context parallelism, and Stage 2 adapts only local memories to smaller chunks for better inference resolution. The claimed ingredients are indeed present in the paper, and the review correctly notes that additive composition is defined, albeit only clearly in the appendix (Eq. 15 / Appendix E). The ablations also support that the main design choices matter: removing global memory hurts badly (Table 3: 21.04→25.60 ppl, 40.6→35.5 acc), removing Q-K projection hurts materially, and Stage 2 gives a modest but real improvement. So the key strengths are real.

The review’s main criticisms are also valid. Most importantly, the abstract says “evaluated on Titans and TTT,” and the framing repeatedly calls TNT a general training paradigm for deep memory modules, but the actual experiments section explicitly says they instantiate with Titans. There is no empirical TTT validation in the visible results. So the breadth claim is definitely overstated relative to evidence. Likewise, the strongest “removes a critical scalability barrier / practical foundation” language goes beyond what 150M / 10B-token evidence can fully support. This is strong proof-of-concept evidence, but not yet decisive evidence of broad scalability.

I also agree with the review that the reset approximation is central and under-analyzed. The paper does show that global memory is necessary when local memories are reset, but it does not directly probe boundary effects or dependency degradation as information crosses resets. The Q-K projection overhead concern is also legitimate: Appendix C explicitly maintains a \(d\times d\) matrix state per local memory. Calling that “constant-size” is true in sequence length, but not in model width; the paper does not quantify that cost.

That said, none of these are fatal. Unlike the PARDIFF calibration example, there is no internal contradiction severe enough to destroy trust in the results. Unlike LLEOT, there is no central theorem/algorithm mismatch that undermines the core claims. And unlike low-scoring efficiency papers whose practical claims rest mostly on proxy accounting, this paper does present direct time-to-target-loss and runtime evidence, plus performance gains, plus ablations.

Relative to calibration:

- It is **much stronger than PARDIFF (0.5)** and also clearly above **SUSI (2.0)**, both of which had major credibility/support gaps that materially undercut their main claims.
- It is also stronger than **PSR (3.2)** and **LLEOT (3.3)**, where overclaiming and limited support bit much harder into the paper’s core narrative.
- Compared with **µLO / MULO (5.0)**, TNT feels somewhat stronger empirically on its main thesis: it has a cleaner practical story, stronger ablations on the central mechanism, and a more directly useful systems-facing contribution. The main limitation is scope/generalization, not whether the proposed mechanism works.
- Compared with **SFDD (5.5)**, TNT is in a similar band: both are strongest as mechanism-backed practical recipes with real empirical gains, but both overstate generality somewhat. TNT’s architectural contribution feels somewhat deeper than SFDD’s filtering heuristic, though SFDD had broader robustness checks.
- Compared with **TempFlow-GRPO (7.5)**, TNT is weaker. TempFlow had broader empirical support across setups/backbones; TNT’s evidence is concentrated on one model family and one modest scale, with stronger extrapolative rhetoric than support.

So this looks like a **solid accept-level paper, but not a top-tier one**. The core idea is novel enough, the empirical improvements are real, and the paper makes deep memory modules more trainable in practice. The main deductions come from overclaiming breadth/generality, limited scale, and insufficient analysis of the reset approximation and Q-K overhead.

