The consolidated review is broadly fair, and after checking the paper text, the most important criticisms are indeed real rather than speculative.

First, the paper’s core mechanism is exactly-token matching over `<feature_name, value>` pairs. The method section defines tokens as string concatenations of feature name and raw value (lines 219–225), retrieves candidates by exact token lookup (227–247), and scores them by overlap count plus an “energy” tie-breaker (250–297). I do not see any principled numeric similarity mechanism: no binning, tolerance, distance kernel, or embedding for nearby continuous values. That makes the review’s main concern about brittleness on continuous tabular data quite strong. The paper explicitly claims flexibility “from numerical to text, without any prior preprocessing” in the conclusion (589–590), which makes the omission more damaging, not less.

Second, the regression formulation is genuinely problematic. The paper says for regression the energy includes `success(NV)^2 / use(NV) * exp(-alpha * MAE(NV))` (280–289), but then states that for regression “a prediction is correct if the predicted value is identical to the current value” (290–292). For real-valued targets this is an extremely brittle notion of success, and the paper does not repair the conceptual mismatch. Since one of the three main tasks is regression, this is not a minor edge case.

Third, the efficiency story is overstated in the paper itself. The paper’s computational claims rely heavily on hand-derived FLOP counts and simplified assumptions (522–550), including scaling two datasets’ cost just by sample-count ratios (549–550). That is far weaker than direct runtime/memory profiling. This matters because the conclusion and discussion make very strong claims: four orders of magnitude less than tree ensembles and six orders less than neural nets (547–548). The review is right that the evidence does not support that magnitude of conclusion.

Fourth, the empirical support is narrow. The main experiments are only three small datasets on a single 60/20/20 split (383–394), with simple baseline setups (407–418). The large-dataset comparison is not a fresh, rigorous benchmark campaign; it partly imports published numbers and then adds the proposed method, with suspiciously huge standard deviations mentioned in the review. I did not read the raw table cells here, but the text around Table 4 clearly shows the large-scale evidence is limited and mostly framed as a speed/accuracy tradeoff rather than robust competitiveness (565–580).

There is one point where the review is slightly too harsh in wording: it says the paper claims no preprocessing for Neurovectors while the experimental section says the Absenteeism dataset was scaled (392). But that inconsistency actually hurts the paper further, since the text simultaneously claims “no preprocessing” for the proposed method in other places (402–403, 514–516, 589–590). So overall the review’s criticism of the preprocessing/numerical-handling story is still justified.

On strengths, the review is also fair. The method is transparent and easy to inspect; it is a concrete non-backprop memory/retrieval procedure; and only storing mispredicted examples is a real design choice (303–317). So this is not a nonsense paper. But the core issue is that the central matching rule seems ill-suited to the very class of tabular problems the paper wants to cover.

Relative to calibration examples:

- This paper is **stronger than COM** (avg 0.0), because COM effectively lacked results and had an incoherent objective. Neurovectors at least specifies an operational algorithm and shows experiments.
- It is probably **stronger than TabPalooza** (avg 1.2) as a “paper artifact” because this submission is at least a complete method paper rather than a benchmark whose main evaluation target is arguably invalid. But TabPalooza’s fatal issue was also central and severe.
- It feels **comparable to or somewhat weaker than the Drosophila online-fitting paper** (avg 2.0) in overall acceptability. That paper had a real systems contribution with overinterpreted science; here the paper has a simple method with a more fundamental mismatch between claimed generality and actual mechanism.
- It is **well below PSR** (avg 3.2), where the main issue was overclaiming and limited robustness evidence despite a technically meaningful, reasonably supported method.
- It is **well below MetaOCDN** (avg 5.0), which had serious theory issues but still broader experiments and a more substantial method.
- It is **far below cLUT** (avg 7.0), which is exactly the opposite on efficiency evaluation: cLUT made careful systems claims with strong measurement, while this paper makes aggressive efficiency claims with weak evidence.
- It is maybe in the same rough reject band as **PARDIFF** (avg 0.5), though for a different reason. PARDIFF had contradictory empirical reporting that broke trust in its main claims. Here the main issue is that the core algorithm seems fundamentally brittle for continuous inputs and regression as formulated.

The decisive question is whether this crosses the “fundamental issue” threshold. I think it nearly does, because the exact-match tokenization and exact-equality regression success criterion undermine the claimed applicability to general tabular classification/regression, especially with numerical data. However, unlike a clearly unfinished or internally contradictory paper, the method is at least executable and might work in settings with repeated values / categorical-heavy data. So I would not go all the way to a near-zero score. Still, the central claims are not technically supported enough for anything close to borderline.

My calibrated score is therefore in the **low reject range**, around **2.4**: below papers that are sound-but-overclaimed, above the truly broken/empty submissions.

