The paper is clearly aiming at a strong theory contribution in an interesting niche: offline robust learning in tabular two-player zero-sum Markov games with partial coverage. After checking the paper, several strengths from the consolidated review are real:

- the setting is genuinely nontrivial and not just a repackaging of standard offline RL or robust RL;
- the \(S(A+B)\) dependence is indeed the advertised structural improvement in the theorem and lower bound;
- the clipped concentrability idea is present and is a meaningful analytical device;
- the paper does explicitly track uncertainty-level dependence and does provide a lower bound.

So this is not a vacuous or purely cosmetic theory paper.

However, the key issue is that the paper itself also supports the most serious weaknesses in the review, especially the ones about the target equilibrium concept and the algorithm-output justification.

Most importantly, the formulation is indeed asymmetric. Equations (3) and (4) define two different robustifications: the max-player uses an inf over one uncertainty set, the min-player a sup over another potentially different one. Then the paper claims existence of policies that “simultaneously achieve” the associated robust best-response values, but in the main text there is no real explanation of why this yields a coherent single zero-sum game object. That matters because the whole guarantee is phrased as learning an \(\varepsilon\)-robust NE.

The algorithmic issue is also real on the face of the main paper. Algorithm 2 computes Nash policies separately for \(\hat Q_h^+\) and \(\hat Q_h^-\), but then outputs the cross-pair \((\hat\mu,\hat\nu)=(\mu^-,\nu^+)\). I do not see a main-text lemma explaining why this cross-combination is an approximate equilibrium for the stated notion. Given that this is exactly the returned policy pair in the theorem, this is not a minor presentation lapse; it strikes at the trustworthiness of the central claim.

There are also several secondary issues that reduce confidence:

- the paper overclaims “optimal/tight” despite explicitly admitting an \(H\)-gap;
- notation and formula inconsistencies are real in the extracted text, including \(C_\epsilon^*\) vs \(C_r^*\), inconsistent \(f(\sigma^+,\sigma^-,H)\), and mismatch between Eq. (20) and Algorithm 2’s penalty;
- the partial-coverage framing is somewhat overstated because Theorem 1 still requires a burn-in depending on \(d_m^n\), the smallest positive occupancy mass;
- the general-sum extension is just a theorem statement plus slogan-level claims in the main paper.

Against calibration:

- This seems weaker than **preference_elicitation_for_offline_reinforcement_learning** (avg 6.8), where the theory had caveats and some overclaiming, but the core object still looked coherent and the paper also had empirical support.
- It is closer in spirit to **guided_reinforcement_learning_with_rollback** (avg 3.0), where the main guarantee was undermined by a mismatch between the formal guarantee and the actual practical object. Here, the mismatch may be even more central because it affects the very definition of the learned equilibrium and the returned pair.
- Compared with **median_clipping_for_zerothorder...** (avg 5.5), that paper had real theory and weaker empirical support, but its core theorem object appeared much more internally coherent. This submission has a potentially more fundamental correctness concern.
- It is stronger than a paper like **Tabby** (avg 3.0) in terms of intellectual ambition and nontrivial technical content, but Tabby’s issues were mostly evidential/confounding; here the concern is closer to whether the theorem is proving the right thing about the right algorithmic output.
- **Satisficing regret minimization in bandits** (avg 6.7) is another useful contrast: there the main criticism was that the reduction was under-justified in the main paper, but the conceptual target still looked coherent. Here the target itself is less convincingly defined.

So I do think the consolidated review is basically fair, and I agree with treating the equilibrium-definition / cross-pairing issue as severe. I would not go all the way to “not even a paper”: there is substantial technical work here, and the sample-complexity direction is interesting. But because the central guarantee is tied to a solution concept and output construction that are not convincingly justified in the main paper, this lands as a fairly clear reject.

